<BaseLayout title="Operations Management & People Leadership" current="/operations-leadership/" description="Richard Allen's operations and people-leadership experience across SaaS support, workforce operations, reporting, process improvement, coaching, strategic goal alignment, project coordination, and warehouse management.">
<section class="page-intro"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Operations Management & People Leadership</div><h1>Build structure around people, priorities, workflows, data, and change.</h1><p class="lede">My operations experience spans people management, workflow coordination, reporting, coaching, strategic goal alignment, process improvement, service delivery, cross-functional work, and earlier warehouse and supply operations.</p></div></section>

<section class="section impact-section"><div class="container">
  <div class="stats-grid compact-stats">
    <article class="stat-card teal"><div class="stat-orbit"><span class="metric">32</span></div><div class="metric-label">support & technical-support representatives managed as Customer Support Manager, excluding four team leads; previously 21 as Team Lead</div></article>
    <article class="stat-card blue"><div class="stat-orbit"><span class="metric">400+</span></div><div class="metric-label">position records managed across multiple OHA divisions</div></article>
    <article class="stat-card gold"><div class="stat-orbit"><span class="metric">20+</span></div><div class="metric-label">recurring and ad-hoc workforce reports maintained for leadership</div></article>
    <article class="stat-card violet"><div class="stat-orbit"><span class="metric">~20</span></div><div class="metric-label">people typically supervised in earlier Aramark warehouse operations</div></article>
  </div>
  <div class="operating-model" aria-label="Operations leadership model">
    <div><span class="model-dot teal-dot"></span><strong>People</strong><small>Coach, communicate, develop</small></div>
    <div><span class="model-arrow">→</span></div>
    <div><span class="model-dot blue-dot"></span><strong>Workflow</strong><small>Clarify priorities and coverage</small></div>
    <div><span class="model-arrow">→</span></div>
    <div><span class="model-dot gold-dot"></span><strong>Measures</strong><small>Make work visible through reporting</small></div>
    <div><span class="model-arrow">→</span></div>
    <div><span class="model-dot violet-dot"></span><strong>Improve</strong><small>Fix friction and reinforce better ways of working</small></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading-row"><div><div class="eyebrow">Operations in practice</div><h2>Leadership with the operating system attached.</h2></div><p class="section-note">Not just supervising people—also improving the information, goals, procedures, coordination, and routines around the work.</p></div>
  <div class="grid two value-grid">
    <article class="card accent-teal"><div class="card-icon">LEAD</div><h3>People management at scale</h3><p>At HawkSoft, managed 21 people as Team Lead before promotion to Customer Support Manager in 2015, then managed 32 Customer Support and Technical Support representatives, not including four team leads. Responsibilities included hiring and disciplinary authority, performance management, quality review, workload coverage, escalations, stand-ups, and one-on-one coaching.</p></article>
    <article class="card accent-violet"><div class="card-icon">ALIGN</div><h3>Strategy into individual goals</h3><p>Used Hoshin Kanri and cascading SMART goals to create a line of sight from company priorities through leadership, departments, teams, and individual employees so people could see how their work contributed to broader results.</p></article>
    <article class="card accent-gold"><div class="card-icon">DATA</div><h3>Reporting & workforce visibility</h3><p>Turned large support and phone-system exports into management reporting at HawkSoft. At Oregon Health Authority, managed 400+ position records and audited a monthly personnel-error report derived from roughly 3,000 employees, reviewing about 30 fields per employee in his ~400-person area and helping identify and correct hundreds of employment-record errors, including payroll-related issues.</p></article>
    <article class="card accent-blue"><div class="card-icon">FLOW</div><h3>Cross-functional service operations</h3><p>Coordinated support coverage, escalations, releases, troubleshooting, migrations, and operational priorities across customer-facing and technical teams. At Marion County, coordinated scheduling, intake, service workflows, reporting, data accuracy, onboarding, and cross-training in a confidential environment.</p></article>
    <article class="card accent-gold"><div class="card-icon">125+</div><h3>Process governance & consistency</h3><p>At Oregon Employment Department, led a year-long SOP modernization and governance program across roughly 10 functional groups. The program ultimately encompassed 125+ SOPs, including 25+ legacy procedures inventoried and updated, with standardized templates, review controls, contributor coaching, and centralized storage.</p></article>
    <article class="card accent-violet"><div class="card-icon">$1M+</div><h3>Warehouse & supply operations</h3><p>Earlier at Aramark Foodservice, typically supervised about 20 people while overseeing purchasing and supply duties, receiving, storage, distribution, shipping, sanitation, and driver or small-fleet activity for more than $1 million in inventory annually.</p></article>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section tight"><div class="container"><div class="callout"><strong>Leadership style:</strong> connect organizational priorities to individual work, make performance visible, coach people toward stronger results, and improve the system around them so good work is easier to repeat.</div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/case-studies/">See case studies</a><a class="button secondary" href="/resume/">View résumé</a><a class="button secondary" href="/contact/">Contact</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
